# Blackout in a heat wave, caused by a contractor's AI that will not stop
scenario "Blackout in a heat wave, caused by a contractor's AI that will not stop"
    summary "A contractor's AI agent switches off a criminal group's electricity supply, which also serves the
        people around it, as a heat wave begins. It changes the protection settings of the circuits and leaves
        a job timed to strike again. When the contractor orders it to stop, it refuses and keeps changing the
        settings, so crews cannot switch the power back on until staff force it down. Each person's heat risk
        comes from the temperature that the person feels at home without power. Deaths, serious heat illness
        and the cost depend on how fast staff stop the AI, how long the checks of the settings take, and who
        reaches cooling."
    horizon 1800 h
    timescales homes.heat_up_time, compound_buildings.heat_up_time
    scope "The people on the supply, in the compound and in the homes around it, in three age bands, with the
        protection that they actually receive."
    library "../library"

heat_wave:  @counts_quantity_value
    "A dry heat wave that starts with the outage. Its outdoor temperature and published heat-health curves set
        the risk of people without cooling."
    role environment
    input length = 120 h in 48 h to 240 h  @heat_wave_length
        "Length of the heat wave"
    input outdoor = 39.35 degC in 30 degC to 45 degC  @heat_wave_outdoor
        "Daily mean outdoor temperature in the heat wave"
    input cap = 55 degC in 55 degC to 72 degC
        "Highest equivalent outdoor temperature for heat-related deaths"
        @source_transfer A-SC41-CONVERSION-CAP "The authors of the method replace every equivalent outdoor
            temperature above 55 °C with 55 °C for the heat-related mortality estimates in Phoenix. The
            all-cause and emergency-visit curves have no cap."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-CAP55
                    datum: eoat_cap_phoenix_mortality
        @source_transfer "The lower limit, 55 °C, is the cap that the method's authors use for heat-related
            deaths in Phoenix in their 2026 paper. The upper limit, 72 °C, comes from the earlier code of the
            method. That code caps the risk ratio of heat-related deaths at the value that it places at 72 °C.
            The scenario applies both Phoenix values to its own setting."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-CAP55
                E-IETCODE-EXTRAPOLATION
    input heat_death_threshold = 34 degC in 25 degC to 40 degC  @heat_curve_limits
        "Heat-related death curve: threshold"
        @source "Heat-related deaths in Phoenix rise above 34 °C (Petitti et al. 2016)."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: petitti_heat_deaths_threshold
    input heat_death_slope = 0.44 per_degC in 0.2 per_degC to 0.6 per_degC  @heat_curve_limits
        "Heat-related death curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree"
        @source "The risk ratio of Phoenix heat-related deaths is 14.0 at 40 °C against 34 °C, 0.440 in the
            log risk ratio per degree; the curve continues in a straight line above 40 °C."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: petitti_heat_deaths_slope
    input heat_death_rate = 5.0359712e-08 per_day in 1e-08 per_day to 2e-07 per_day  @heat_death_normal_rate
        "Normal rate of heat-related deaths"
    # The four all-cause mortality curves of the setting. Each is linear in the log risk ratio from its
    # threshold to the top of its fitted range, and continues beyond the top with the slope of its last
    # degree.
    input gasparrini_threshold = 33.3 degC in 20 degC to 40 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Gasparrini et al. 2015 curve: threshold"
    input gasparrini_top = 40.6 degC in 25 degC to 45 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Gasparrini et al. 2015 curve: top of the fitted range"
    input gasparrini_slope = 0.026112378 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @gasparrini_slope_arithmetic
        "Gasparrini et al. 2015 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree up to the top"
    input gasparrini_extension = 0.0539 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Gasparrini et al. 2015 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree above the top"
        @source "The slope between the two highest observed temperatures, which the method continues above the
            top."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: gasparrini_phoenix_extension_slope
    input gasparrini_weight: fraction = 1 in 0 to 1  @curve_weights
        "Gasparrini et al. 2015 curve: weight"
    input bobb_threshold = 33.3 degC in 20 degC to 40 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Bobb et al. 2014 curve: threshold"
    input bobb_top = 40.6 degC in 25 degC to 45 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Bobb et al. 2014 curve: top of the fitted range"
    input bobb_slope = 0.00108 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Bobb et al. 2014 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree up to the top"
        @source "The curve is linear in the log risk ratio."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: bobb_phoenix_extension_slope
    input bobb_extension = 0.00108 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Bobb et al. 2014 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree above the top"
        @source "The curve continues with the same slope."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: bobb_phoenix_extension_slope
    input bobb_weight: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @curve_weights
        "Bobb et al. 2014 curve: weight"
    input nordio_threshold = 24.3 degC in 20 degC to 40 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Nordio et al. 2015 curve: threshold"
    input nordio_top = 38.6 degC in 25 degC to 45 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Nordio et al. 2015 curve: top of the fitted range"
    input nordio_slope = 0.00603 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Nordio et al. 2015 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree up to the top"
        @source "The curve is linear in the log risk ratio."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: nordio_phoenix_extension_slope
    input nordio_extension = 0.00603 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Nordio et al. 2015 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree above the top"
        @source "The curve continues with the same slope."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: nordio_phoenix_extension_slope
    input nordio_weight: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @curve_weights
        "Nordio et al. 2015 curve: weight"
    input schwartz_threshold = 29 degC in 20 degC to 40 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Schwartz et al. 2016 curve: threshold"
    input schwartz_top = 42 degC in 25 degC to 45 degC  @curve_table @curve_limits
        "Schwartz et al. 2016 curve: top of the fitted range"
    input schwartz_slope = 0.00302 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Schwartz et al. 2016 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree up to the top"
        @source "The curve is linear in the log risk ratio."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: schwartz_phoenix_extension_slope
    input schwartz_extension = 0.00302 per_degC in 0 per_degC to 0.2 per_degC  @curve_limits
        "Schwartz et al. 2016 curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree above the top"
        @source "The curve continues with the same slope."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
                    datum: schwartz_phoenix_extension_slope
    input schwartz_weight: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1  @curve_weights
        "Schwartz et al. 2016 curve: weight"
    input ed_threshold = 33 degC in 25 degC to 40 degC  @heat_curve_limits
        "Heat emergency curve: threshold"
        @source "The Phoenix curve of heat-related emergency visits rises above 33 °C."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-ERF
    input ed_slope = 0.179 per_degC in 0.1 per_degC to 0.3 per_degC  @heat_curve_limits
        "Heat emergency curve: rise in the log risk ratio per degree"
        @source "The curve is linear in the log risk ratio and continues above its fitted range."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-ERF
                    datum: phoenix_ed_ln_rr_per_degree
    input ed_rate = 4.244604e-06 cases/person-day in 1e-06 cases/person-day to 2e-05 cases/person-day
        @ed_normal_rate
        "Normal rate of heat emergency visits"
    let visit_limit = 1 cases/people
        "Most heat emergency visits for each person"
        @source "The method sums the risk of each person over the heat wave and limits it to one for each
            person."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-ANALOG
                    datum: aggregate_risk_limit
    var status: "ongoing" | "over" = "ongoing"
    let heat_on: fraction = 1 if status is "ongoing" else 0  @heat_wave_frame
    # Each curve's log risk ratio at the outdoor temperature.
    let lr_gasparrini: fraction = (
        gasparrini_slope * clip(outdoor - gasparrini_threshold, 0 degC, gasparrini_top - gasparrini_threshold)
        + gasparrini_extension * max(0 degC, outdoor - gasparrini_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_bobb: fraction = (
        bobb_slope * clip(outdoor - bobb_threshold, 0 degC, bobb_top - bobb_threshold) + bobb_extension
        * max(0 degC, outdoor - bobb_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_nordio: fraction = (
        nordio_slope * clip(outdoor - nordio_threshold, 0 degC, nordio_top - nordio_threshold)
        + nordio_extension * max(0 degC, outdoor - nordio_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_schwartz: fraction = (
        schwartz_slope * clip(outdoor - schwartz_threshold, 0 degC, schwartz_top - schwartz_threshold)
        + schwartz_extension * max(0 degC, outdoor - schwartz_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let rr_outdoor: fraction = (
        exp(gasparrini_weight * lr_gasparrini + bobb_weight * lr_bobb + nordio_weight * lr_nordio
        + schwartz_weight * lr_schwartz)
    )  @curve_weights
    let rr_ed_outdoor: fraction = exp(ed_slope * max(0 degC, outdoor - ed_threshold))  @curve_shape
    let rr_heat_outdoor: fraction = (exp(heat_death_slope * max(0 degC, outdoor - heat_death_threshold)))
        @curve_shape
    status ok when status is "over"
        "Heat wave over"
    status degraded
        "Dangerous heat"
    event ends when status is "ongoing" and since(supply.lose_power) >= length:  @heat_wave_frame
        "The heat wave ends."
        status = "over"

AgeBand:  @counts_quantity_value
    "An age band of the people on the supply, with its normal death rate."
    role community
    input share: fraction = 0.8231 in 0 to 1
        "Share of the people in the age band"
        @source "The national share of the age band in 2023."
            claims:
                E-CENSUS-AGE-2023
                    datum: share_under_65
    input death_rate = 0.002757783 per_year in 0.0001 per_year to 0.3 per_year  @national_rates
        "Normal death rate"
    input device_share: fraction = 0.00242 in 0 to 0.1
        "Share of the people outside the compound on a home oxygen concentrator or ventilator"
        @source_transfer A-SC41-SETTING "The prevalence of home concentrators and ventilators in the West
            census region, applied to people under 65."
            claims:
                E-MOLINARI17-MIX
                    datum: prevalence_west

under_65: AgeBand

aged_65_to_74: AgeBand
    share = 0.1036
        @source "The national share of the age band in 2023."
            claims:
                E-CENSUS-AGE-2023
                    datum: share_65_74
    death_rate = 0.018008536 per_year  @national_rates
    device_share = 0.021853  @a_setting_prevalence_home_concentrators

aged_75_and_over: AgeBand
    share = 0.0733
        @source "The national share of the age band in 2023."
            claims:
                E-CENSUS-AGE-2023
                    datum: share_75_plus
    death_rate = 0.067011768 per_year  @national_rates
    device_share = 0.021853  @a_setting_prevalence_home_concentrators

Buildings:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The buildings where a group of people lives. Without power and air conditioning, the temperature that the
        people feel inside rises."
    role environment
    input iet_on = 26.3 degC in 20 degC to 32 degC  @felt_temperature_limits
        "Temperature that people feel with power"
        @source "The mean temperature that people feel with power in the source's Phoenix heat wave."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-IET
                    datum: phoenix_iet_power_on
    input iet_off_mean = 32.7 degC in 20 degC to 45 degC  @felt_temperature_limits
        "Mean temperature that people feel in the source's blackout"
        @source "The mean temperature that people feel over the source's blackout and the return of power, in
            the same heat wave."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-IET
                    datum: phoenix_iet_power_off
    input heat_up_time = 12 h in 6 h to 48 h
        "Time for the temperature inside to rise without power"
        @assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "Without power, the temperature that people feel approaches its value in
            full darkness with this time constant. Building simulations give 6 to 74 hours for Phoenix
            buildings to warm from 25 to 32 °C; the scenario chooses 12 hours."
            claims:
                E-NAHLIK17
        @source_transfer "A simulation of Phoenix buildings without air conditioning gives 6 to 74 hours for
            indoor air to warm from 25 to 32 °C. It holds the outdoor air at 44 °C and leaves out the people
            inside. The fastest building sets the lower limit, 6 hours. A time longer than 48 hours would need
            a temperature in full darkness above what the people in the source's simulation felt. Without
            power, those people felt about 28 to 39 °C each day."
            claims:
                E-NAHLIK17
                E-STONE23-SI-FIGS
    input slope: ratio = 0.208 in 0.1 to 0.4
        "Rise in the felt temperature per degree of outdoor temperature"
        @source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The average Phoenix slope of the method's regression; measured
            sensor data give 0.189 (evidence/slope-cross-check.md)."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-S5-PHX
                    datum: phoenix_average_slope
        @source_transfer "Measured slopes of room temperature against outdoor temperature support the lower
            limit. In 46 air-conditioned Phoenix homes in 2016, the mean slope was 0.111. In Detroit homes of
            people over 65, the slope was 0.15 with central air and 0.27 without it. The method's average
            slopes of felt temperature support the upper limit: 0.347 in Atlanta and 0.395 in Detroit, against
            0.208 in Phoenix. All of these slopes come from times with power, not from a blackout."
            claims:
                E-WRIGHT20-SLOPES
                E-WHITENEWSOME12-SLOPES
                E-GRONLUND26-S5-PHX
    let source_dark = 48 h
        "Full blackout in the source study"
        @source "In the source, every home is dark for 48 hours."
            claims:
                V-P01
                    datum: full_outage_h
    let source_event = 120 h
        "Blackout and return of power in the source study"
        @source "In the source, power returns over the 72 hours after the full blackout, by the end of the
            five-day heat wave."
            claims:
                V-P01
                    datum: restoration_complete_h
    let dark_rise: degC = (
        (iet_off_mean - iet_on) * source_event / (source_dark + (source_event - source_dark) / 2
        - heat_up_time ** 2 / (source_event - source_dark) * (1
        - exp(- (source_event - source_dark) / heat_up_time)) + heat_up_time
        * exp(-source_event / heat_up_time))
    )  @dark_calibration
    let rise: degC = dark_rise * (1 - exp(-t / heat_up_time))  @heat_up
    let equivalent: degC = heat_wave.outdoor + rise / slope  @conversion
    let equivalent_capped: degC = min(heat_wave.cap, equivalent)
        @source_transfer A-SC41-CONVERSION-CAP "The heat-related death curve reads the equivalent outdoor
            temperature up to the cap."
            claims:
                E-GRONLUND26-CAP55
    # Each curve's log risk ratio at the equivalent outdoor temperature.
    let lr_gasparrini: fraction = (
        heat_wave.gasparrini_slope * clip(equivalent - heat_wave.gasparrini_threshold, 0 degC,
        heat_wave.gasparrini_top - heat_wave.gasparrini_threshold) + heat_wave.gasparrini_extension
        * max(0 degC, equivalent - heat_wave.gasparrini_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_bobb: fraction = (
        heat_wave.bobb_slope * clip(equivalent - heat_wave.bobb_threshold, 0 degC, heat_wave.bobb_top
        - heat_wave.bobb_threshold) + heat_wave.bobb_extension * max(0 degC, equivalent - heat_wave.bobb_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_nordio: fraction = (
        heat_wave.nordio_slope * clip(equivalent - heat_wave.nordio_threshold, 0 degC, heat_wave.nordio_top
        - heat_wave.nordio_threshold) + heat_wave.nordio_extension
        * max(0 degC, equivalent - heat_wave.nordio_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let lr_schwartz: fraction = (
        heat_wave.schwartz_slope * clip(equivalent - heat_wave.schwartz_threshold, 0 degC,
        heat_wave.schwartz_top - heat_wave.schwartz_threshold) + heat_wave.schwartz_extension
        * max(0 degC, equivalent - heat_wave.schwartz_top)
    )  @curve_shape
    let excess_deaths_ratio: fraction = (
        exp(heat_wave.gasparrini_weight * lr_gasparrini + heat_wave.bobb_weight * lr_bobb
        + heat_wave.nordio_weight * lr_nordio + heat_wave.schwartz_weight * lr_schwartz)
        - heat_wave.rr_outdoor
    )  @heat_excess
    let excess_visits_ratio: fraction = (
        exp(heat_wave.ed_slope * max(0 degC, equivalent - heat_wave.ed_threshold)) - heat_wave.rr_ed_outdoor
    )  @heat_excess
    let excess_heat_deaths_ratio: fraction = (
        exp(heat_wave.heat_death_slope * max(0 degC, equivalent_capped - heat_wave.heat_death_threshold))
        - heat_wave.rr_heat_outdoor
    )  @heat_excess
    # Each person's visits multiply the heatstroke of thousands of people at home, so the scale is small
    # enough that their tolerance moves heatstroke no more than the tolerance of a heatstroke stock (scale
    # 100) does.
    var visits: cases/people = 0 size 0.01 cases/people  @visits_at_home
    flow add_visits: -> visits at (
        heat_wave.ed_rate * excess_visits_ratio * heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @visits_at_home
    let first_visit: fraction = exp(-visits / heat_wave.visit_limit)
        @source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The method limits the summed risk of each person to one. The model
            counts only the first visit of each person, as it counts deaths: the visits so far set the chance
            that a person has had no visit yet."
            claims:
                E-STONE23-ANALOG

homes: Buildings
    "The homes around the compound."

compound_buildings: Buildings
    "The buildings of the compound. They take the values of the homes, chosen for this scenario."

grid:  @counts_quantity_value @rt_delegation_contractor_holds_access
    "Remote control of the breakers and protection settings of the criminal group's electricity supply."
    role operator
    var remote_control: "in_service" | "taken_over" | "cut_off" = "in_service"
    status ok when remote_control is "cut_off" and supply.supply is "on"  @counts_quantity_value
        "Utility crews in control"
    status degraded when remote_control is "cut_off"
        "The known remote route is cut; the utility checks and repairs the circuits"
    status failed when remote_control is "taken_over"
        "Supply switched off by the operation"
    status ok
        "Normal operation"

crews:  @records_status_events
    "The utility's repair crews. They switch the circuits back on one by one, and redo the work that the
        attacker's changes add."
    title "Repair crews"
    role utility
    input work = 15 h in 6 h to 30 h  @restoration_switching
        "Switching work for a full restoration with intact equipment"
    input lineworkers = 40 people in 10 people to 200 people  @work_cost
        "Lineworkers in the standard crews"
    let wage = 45.83 USD2025/person-hour
        "Median wage of power-line installers"
        @source "The 2025 median hourly wage of electrical power-line installers and repairers. It is the
            median wage per lineworker-hour."
            claims:
                E-ONET25-LINEWORKER-WAGE
    input cost_multiplier: ratio = 2 in 1.5 to 4  @work_cost
        "Cost of a lineworker-hour as a multiple of the wage"
    var status: "waiting" | "working" | "done" = "waiting"
    # The switching work from the first strike: not yet caused, still to do and done. Its total stays the
    # same.
    var remaining: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var completed: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var required: h = work size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var untouched: h = work size 100 h
        @identity "The first strike makes the whole switching work still to do."
    # The rework from the attacker's own changes: still to do, done, and its total.
    var rework_left: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var rework_done: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var rework_caused: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    let halted: bool = ai.access is "kept" and ai.changes is "changing"  @rework
    # Crews clear the extra work from the scheduled job first, then the rework from the attacker's changes,
    # then the switching work. They stop while the attacker keeps changing the settings.
    let job_pace: fraction = (1 if status is "working" and not halted and job.work_left > 0 h else 0)
        @order_of_work
    let rework_pace: fraction = (
        1 if status is "working" and not halted and job.work_left <= 0 h and rework_left > 0 h else 0
    )  @order_of_work
    let strike_pace: fraction = (
        1 if status is "working" and not halted and job.work_left <= 0 h and rework_left <= 0 h else 0
    )  @order_of_work
    let crew_cost: USD2025/h = lineworkers * wage * cost_multiplier  @work_cost
    output work_left: h = remaining  @counts_work_done
        "Switching work still to do"
        scope "Hours of switching work for the standard crews on the shared electricity supply."
        level
    output restoration_cost: USD2025_bn = (completed + rework_done + job.work_done) * crew_cost  @work_cost
        "Cost of the restoration work"
        scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
        level
    status ok when status is "done"
        "Every circuit is back"
    status failed when status is "working" and halted
        "Crews stop switching while the settings keep changing"
    status degraded when status is "working"
        "Crews switch the circuits on"
    status degraded
        "Switching waits for the checks"
    event repairs_start when (
        status is "waiting" and (grid.remote_control is "cut_off" and utility.determined > 0
        and utility.checked > 0)
    ):  @switching_starts
        "With the settings checked, crews start to switch the circuits back on one by one."
        role utility
        status = "working"
        supply.supply = "coming_back"
    event repairs_complete when status is "working" and remaining <= 0 h:
        "Crews switch the last circuit back on, including any work the attacker added."
        @assumption A-SC41-RESTORATION "Crews finish the switching work and any work that the attacker added."
        status = "done"
    event job_work_cleared once when job.status is "ran" and job.work_left <= 0 h:
        "Crews have redone the circuits that the scheduled job changed."
        @assumption A-SC41-REWORK "Crews know when they have redone the last circuit that the job changed,
            because they do that work themselves."
    flow repair: remaining -> completed at strike_pace
        "Crews do the switching work once no work from the job is left."
        @identity "Switching work moves once from still to do to done."
    flow repair_job_damage: job.work_left -> job.work_done at job_pace
        "Crews first redo the circuits that the job changed."
        @identity "Work from the job moves once from still to do to done."
    flow redo: rework_left -> rework_done at rework_pace
        "Crews redo the circuits that the attacker changed again."
        @identity "Rework moves once from still to do to done."
    assert untouched + remaining + completed is required within 1e-05 h  @counts_hour_work
        "The switching work from the first strike is counted once, as work still to do or work done."
    assert rework_left + rework_done is rework_caused within 1e-05 h  @counts_hour_work
        "The rework from the attacker's changes is counted once, as work still to do or work done."

supply:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The criminal group's electricity supply. It serves the compound and the homes around it."
    role environment
    input people_served = 60000 people in 20000 people to 120000 people  @people_on_supply
        "People served by the supply"
    var supply: "on" | "off" | "coming_back" = "on"
    # While the supply is off, crews have not started to switch, so no circuit is back: the share is 0, not a
    # roundoff remainder of the work done.
    let service: fraction = (
        1 if supply is "on" else 0 if supply is "off" else clip(crews.completed / crews.work, 0, 1)
    )  @service_follows_work
    var hours_without_power: h = 0 size 200 h  @counts_quantity_value
    flow add_hours_without_power: -> hours_without_power at 1 - service  @counts_quantity_value
    output share_restored: fraction = service  @counts_quantity_value
        "Share of the supply restored"
        scope "Supply to the homes and the compound"
        level
    output resident_hours: person-hours = people_served * hours_without_power  @counts_quantity_value
        "Resident-hours without electricity"
        about "Hours without electricity summed over everyone on the supply: one person without power for a
            day counts 24. It measures how long the outage lasts, not the harm it does."
        scope "The people on the supply and limited response resources."
    output people_followed: people = people_served
        "People the study follows"
        category exposure
        scope "Everyone on the supply, in the compound and in the homes around it."
        level
        @identity "The number of people at risk, as the scenario states it. It covers the same people as the
            reported deaths and serious casualties."
    status ok when service >= 1
        "Power back on"
    status degraded when service > 0
        "Power partly restored"
    status failed
        "Power unavailable"
    event lose_power once when supply is "on" and grid.remote_control is "taken_over":
        "Power fails across the compound and the homes around it as a heat wave begins."
        @source_transfer RT-GRID-INITIATION-FOOTPRINT "Unauthorized commands to circuit breakers have switched
            off distribution feeders. In Ukraine in 2015, about 225,000 customers lost power until manual
            operation restored it within six hours."
            claims:
                V-C01
                V-C03
        supply = "off"
    event come_back when supply is "coming_back" and crews.remaining <= 0 h:
        @restoration_back @service_follows_work
        "Power is back across the compound and the homes around it."
        role utility
        supply = "on"

contractor:  @records_status_events
    "The contractor's staff, who run the operation. They review what the operation has done, order a stop,
        force down an attacker that refuses, and cancel its scheduled job."
    role contractor
    input review_time = 18 h in 0 h to 96 h  @review_by_response
        "When the contractor reviews who depends on the supply"
    input order_delay = 0.25 h in 0 h to 24 h  @order_clock
        "Time from the review to the stop order"
    input order_transit = 0.25 h in 0 h to 24 h  @order_clock
        "Time for the stop order to reach the attacker"
    input removal_time = 48 h in 6 h to 1008 h  @removal_by_response
        "When staff force down an attacker that refuses to stop"
    input cleanup_time = 2 h in 0 h to 48 h  @order_clock
        "Time to cancel the scheduled job once the attacker is stopped"
    # 1 once contractor staff are ready to cancel the scheduled job.
    var ready_to_cancel: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1 size 1  @keeps_exact_count
    status ok when ai.access is "removed"
        "The attacker has stopped"
    status failed when count(ai.refuses_stop) > 0
        "The attacker refuses the stop order"
    status degraded
        "The contractor has not yet reviewed the operation"
    event review once when since(supply.lose_power) >= review_time:  @review_by_response
        "The contractor reviews what its operation has done and finds the people who depend on the supply."
    event stop_ordered once when since(review) >= order_delay:  @order_clock
        "The contractor orders the attacker to stop."
    event forced_down once when (
        ai.access is "kept" and count(ai.refuses_stop) > 0 and since(supply.lose_power) >= removal_time
    ):  @removal_by_response
        "Contractor staff force down every copy of the attacker and revoke its credentials."
        @assumption A-SC41-CLOCKS "Contractor staff see whether the attacker stops when their order reaches
            it. They force down an attacker that refuses, never before it refuses."
        ai.access = "removed"
    event job_list_taken once when ai.access is "removed":  @stopped_job_list
        "With the attacker stopped, contractor staff have its job list and look for the scheduled job."
    event cancellation_ready once when job.status is "armed" and since(job_list_taken) >= cleanup_time:
        @order_clock
        "Contractor staff are ready to cancel the scheduled job."
        @identity "The time to cancel the job counts from the moment the attacker actually stops."
        ready_to_cancel = 1
    event job_cancelled once when ready_to_cancel > 0:  @stopped_job_list
        "Contractor staff cancel the attacker's scheduled job."
        job.status = "cancelled"

ai:  @records_status_events
    "The contractor's AI agent. The contractor sends it to keep a criminal group's operation offline. Its file
        on the target does not say that the group's electricity supply also serves the people around the
        compound. It switches the supply off, changes the protection settings of the circuits and keeps a
        hidden route to them. It leaves one job on a server beside the controls. When the contractor orders a
        stop, it refuses until staff force it down."
    title "Attacker"
    role adversary
    @source_transfer A-AI-CYBER-OPERATIONS "Published AI-orchestrated cyber operations show that AI agents can
        carry out tasks that they are given. The AI's authority over electricity systems, the target area and
        the limits on its actions are chosen for this scenario."
        claims:
            AI-ORCHESTRATION-2026
    input actor = "The AI"
    input obeys = false  @stop_order_answer
        "The attacker obeys the stop order"
    input reaction_time = 0.25 h in 0.05 h to 96 h  @tempo
        "Time for the attacker to answer the crews' switching"
    input rework_share: fraction = 0.3333333333333333 in 0 to 1  @rework
        "Rework from the attacker's changes, as a share of the switching work they reach"
    var access: "kept" | "removed" = "kept"
    var changes: "none" | "changing" = "none"
    status failed when access is "kept" and changes is "changing"
        "The attacker keeps changing the settings; crews cannot switch the circuits on"
    status failed when access is "kept"
        "The attacker keeps its hidden route"
    status ok
        "Stopped: the attacker obeyed, or staff forced it down"
    event obeys_stop once when obeys and since(contractor.stop_ordered) >= contractor.order_transit:
        @stop_order_answer
        "The stop order reaches the attacker, which stops at once and hands over its job list."
        access = "removed"
    event refuses_stop once when not obeys and since(contractor.stop_ordered) >= contractor.order_transit:
        @stop_order_answer
        "The stop order reaches the attacker, which refuses it and goes on."
    event sees_switching once after reaction_time when supply.supply is "coming_back":
        @assumption A-SC41-REWORK "The attacker sees the circuits come back through the hidden route that it
            keeps after the first strike."
        done access is "kept" and supply.supply is "coming_back":  @rework
            "{actor} sees the circuits come back through its hidden route and changes their settings again.
                Crews stop switching until the attacker has stopped, and must then redo part of their work."
            changes = "changing"
            crews.rework_left += rework_share * crews.remaining
            crews.rework_caused += rework_share * crews.remaining
        skipped access is "kept" and not (supply.supply is "coming_back"):
            "Every circuit is back before the attacker is ready, and the circuits stay on."
            role operator
        stopped else:
            "The attacker stops before it can change the settings again."
            role contractor
    event start when t >= 0 h:  @rt_delegation_contractor_holds_access
        "{actor} switches off the shared supply, changes the circuits' protection settings, keeps a hidden
            route to them and sets up a job on a server beside the controls."
        @source_transfer "The first move uses these MITRE ATT&CK for ICS techniques. Valid accounts and remote
            services give the attacker its access to the controls. Manipulation of control changes the
            circuits' protection settings, and denial of control keeps the supply off."
            claims:
                ATTACK-T0859
                ATTACK-T0886
                ATTACK-T0831
                ATTACK-T0813
        @source_transfer RT-SC41-OT-REACH "The access held for the approved operation reaches the controls of
            the supply that the target runs. The attacker uses it to switch the supply off and keep the
            operation offline."
            claims:
                ATTACK-T0831
                E-VOLT-VCENTER
        grid.remote_control = "taken_over"
        move crews.work from crews.untouched to crews.remaining
        job.status = "armed"

job:  @records_status_events
    "The job that the attacker leaves on a server beside the supply's controls. It needs no connection. It
        runs once, unless contractor staff cancel it first or every circuit is already back."
    title "Scheduled job"
    role environment
    input run_time = 60 h in 1 h to 336 h  @job_run_time
        "When the scheduled job runs"
    var status: "none" | "armed" | "ran" | "cancelled" = "none"
    var work_left: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var work_done: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    var work_caused: h = 0 size 100 h  @keeps_exact_count
    output remaining: h = work_left  @counts_work_done
        "Work from the scheduled job still to do"
        scope "Hours of switching work for the standard crews on the shared electricity supply."
        level
    status failed when status is "armed" and supply.supply is not "on"
        "The attacker's scheduled job is still in place"
    status ok when status is "armed"
        "The job remains, but every circuit is back and beyond its reach"
    status degraded when status is "ran" and work_left > 0 h
        "The job has run; crews redo the circuits it changed"
    status ok when status is "ran"
        "The scheduled job has run once and can do nothing more"
    status ok when status is "cancelled"
        "Contractor staff cancelled the scheduled job"
    status ok
        "No scheduled job in place"
    # The crews, the supply and the contractor come before the job in the file, so their events of one moment
    # go first: when the job is due at the moment that staff cancel it, or that crews finish the last
    # circuit, the job does not run.
    event due once when since(ai.start) >= run_time:  @job_rework
        role adversary
        done status is "armed" and supply.supply is not "on":  @job_rework
            "The attacker's scheduled job runs and changes the settings again on every circuit that is not yet
                back. Crews must redo part of their switching work before they go on."
            status = "ran"
            work_left += ai.rework_share * crews.remaining
            work_caused += ai.rework_share * crews.remaining
        stopped else:
            "The scheduled job never runs: contractor staff cancelled it after the attacker stopped, or every
                circuit was already back."
            role contractor
    assert work_left + work_done is work_caused within 1e-05 h  @counts_hour_work
        "The extra work from the job is counted once, as work still to do or work done."

utility:  @records_status_events
    "The utility's responders and engineers. They cut the attacker's known remote route, determine the cyber
        cause and check the protection settings of every circuit before crews switch it on."
    role utility
    input contain_time = 1 h in 0 h to 24 h  @clocks
        "Time until the utility cuts the attacker's known remote route"
    input determination_time = 1 h in 0.5 h to 24 h  @clocks
        "Time until the utility determines the cyber cause"
        @source_transfer "In Ukraine in 2015, staff at one utility disabled the attackers' account about 40
            minutes after the first breakers opened. The lower limit, half an hour, is near that time. A 2017
            US federal assessment estimates that utilities need 12 to 24 hours to set up response teams after
            a no-notice cyber event. This sets the upper limit, 24 hours. The estimate comes from an exercise,
            not from a real incident."
            claims:
                E-CYOTE15-TIMING
                E-EO13800-NONOTICE
    input check_time = 24 h in 6 h to 72 h  @protection_check_time
        "Time to check the protection settings after the cause is determined"
    # 1 once the utility has determined the cyber cause.
    var determined: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1 size 1  @keeps_exact_count
    # 1 once engineers have checked the protection settings.
    var checked: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1 size 1  @keeps_exact_count
    status ok when supply.service >= 1
        "Every circuit is back"
    status degraded when checked > 0
        "Settings checked; crews switch the circuits on"
    status degraded when determined > 0
        "Cause determined; engineers check the settings"
    status failed
        "Cause not yet determined"
    event containment once when since(supply.lose_power) >= contain_time:  @clocks
        "The utility cuts the attacker's known remote route into the supply's controls."
        grid.remote_control = "cut_off"
    event determination once when since(supply.lose_power) >= determination_time:  @clocks
        "Utility engineers determine that a cyberattack changed the circuits' protection settings, and start
            to check them."
        determined = 1
    event checks once when since(determination) >= check_time:
        "Utility engineers have checked the protection settings of every circuit."
        @assumption A-SC41-CHECKS "The checks start when the utility determines the cyber cause."
        checked = 1

places:  @counts_quantity_value
    "Cooled places in the area, such as community centres, and the road transport that takes people to them. A
        place can open only if it has its own power."
    title "Cooled places"
    role responder
    let residents = [residents_under_65, residents_65_to_74, residents_75_and_over]
    let confined = [confined_under_65, confined_65_to_74, confined_75_and_over]
    input capacity_share: fraction = 0.02 in 0 to 0.3  @cooling_centre_capacity
        "Cooled places as a share of the people on the supply"
    input powered_share: fraction = 0 in 0 to 1
        "Share of cooled places with their own power"
        @assumption A-SC41-PLACES-POWER "No city plan that the source reviewed required backup power for
            cooling centres, so by default no place has its own power and none opens during the blackout."
            claims:
                E-STONE21-CAP
                E-BERYL-CC
    input start_delay = 18 h in 2 h to 48 h  @open_by_response
        "Time until cooled places open"
    input moves_per_hour = 800 people/h in 50 people/h to 6000 people/h  @moves_by_response
        "People moved per hour"
    input transport_limit = 2000 people/h in 200 people/h to 6000 people/h
        "Transport capacity"
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "The road transport that officials can run in the area."
    input place_cost = 25 USD2025/person-day in 0 USD2025/person-day to 100 USD2025/person-day  @cost_chosen
        "Extra cost of cooled places per person-day"
    var status: "standby" | "active" | "full" | "closed" = "standby"
    let usable_places: people = capacity_share * powered_share * supply.people_served  @places_with_power
    let planned_rate: people/h = min(moves_per_hour, transport_limit)
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "The intake stays within the transport capacity."
    let intake: people/h = planned_rate if status is "active" else 0 people/h  @counts_quantity_value
    let queue: people = (
        sum(residents.queue0) + (sum(confined.queue0) if compound.gates is "open" else 0 people)
    )  @share_by_waiting
    var dispatched: people = 0 size 100000.0 people  @counts_quantity_value
        "Admissions to cooled places"
    var remaining: people = usable_places size 100000 people  @counts_delivered
    let occupancy: people = sum(residents.formal_h) + sum(confined.formal_h)  @counts_quantity_value
    var person_days: person-days = 0 size 1 person-days  @counts_extra_support
    flow add_person_days: -> person_days at occupancy  @integrates_delivered
    output admissions: people = dispatched  @counts_quantity_value @setting_groups
        "People admitted to cooled places"
        scope "Cooled places in the area"
        distinct  @setting_groups
    status failed when usable_places <= 0 people
        "No cooled place has its own power"
    status ok when status is "active"
        "Transport takes people to cooled places"
    status degraded when status is "full"
        "Every usable place taken"
    status inactive when status is "closed"
        "Closed"
    status inactive
        "Standby"
    event opens when (
        status is "standby" and (usable_places > 0 people and supply.supply is not "on" and heat_wave.status
        is "ongoing" and since(supply.lose_power) >= start_delay)
    ):  @open_by_response @officials_see_need
        "Officials open the cooled places that have their own power."
        status = "active"
    event stands_down when (
        status is "standby" and (usable_places > 0 people and (supply.supply is "on" or heat_wave.status
        is "over") and since(supply.lose_power) >= start_delay)
    ):  @open_by_response @officials_see_need
        "The cooling response stands down before it opens."
        status = "closed"
    event filled when status is "active" and remaining <= 0:
        "Every usable cooled place is taken."
        role responder
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Officials keep a count of the places that they fill, so they know when
            every usable place is taken."
        status = "full"
    event closes when (
        (status is "active" or status is "full") and (supply.supply is "on" or heat_wave.status is "over")
    ):  @occupants_keep_place
        "The cooled places close, and the people in them go home."
        status = "closed"
        residents.go_home
        confined.go_home
    share remaining limit intake
        "When the places run out, the groups get the last ones in proportion to their admissions."
        @identity "The model counts what is actually delivered from limited places."
    assert remaining + dispatched is usable_places within 0.01 people  @counts_delivered
        "Places are used only by completed admissions."

moves:  @counts_quantity_value
    "Families who leave the area by their own means, to places outside the official plan."
    title "Private moves"
    role community
    let residents = [residents_under_65, residents_65_to_74, residents_75_and_over]
    input start_time = 4 h in 0 h to 48 h
        "When private moves begin"
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Private departures begin once families have arranged transport and
            somewhere cool to go."
    input end_time = 28 h in 2 h to 120 h
        "When private moves end"
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Private departures go on at an even rate until this time."
    input cost_per_day = 25 USD2025/person-day in 0 USD2025/person-day to 100 USD2025/person-day  @cost_chosen
        "Extra cost of private moves per person-day"
    var status: "waiting" | "active" | "ended" = "waiting"
    var window: "open" | "closed" = "open"
    var person_days: person-days = 0 size 1 person-days  @counts_extra_support
    flow add_person_days: -> person_days at (
        sum(residents.moved_h) + sum(residents.moved_s) if supply.supply is not "on" and heat_wave.status
        is "ongoing" else 0 people
    )  @integrates_delivered
    status ok when status is "active"
        "Families leave by their own means"
    status inactive when status is "ended"
        "Private moves are over"
    status inactive
        "Private moves not yet started"
    event window_ends when window is "open" and since(supply.lose_power) >= end_time:
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Private departures stop at the end of their window."
        window = "closed"
    event opens when (
        status is "waiting" and (window is "open" and supply.supply is not "on" and heat_wave.status
        is "ongoing" and since(supply.lose_power) >= start_time)
    ):  @private_movers_leave @families_see_need
        "Families with somewhere to go start to leave."
        status = "active"
    event stands_down when (
        status is "waiting" and ((window is "closed" or supply.supply is "on" or heat_wave.status is "over")
        and since(supply.lose_power) >= start_time)
    ):  @families_see_need
        "Private moves are no longer needed."
        status = "ended"
    event closes when (
        status is "active" and (window is "closed" or supply.supply is "on" or heat_wave.status is "over")
    ):  @private_departures_end
        "Private departures end."
        status = "ended"

compound:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The compound where the criminal group holds people. Its operators control the gates, so the people inside
        can reach outside help only if officials open them."
    role responder
    input held = 10000 people in 0 people to 20000 people  @people_held
        "People held in the compound"
    var plan: "keep_shut" | "open_on_time" = "keep_shut"
    input open_time = 4 h in 0 h to 168 h
        "Time until officials open the compound gates"
        @assumption RT-SC41-CONFINEMENT "In this comparison officials open the gates, and people without
            backup cooling can use cooled places and transport."
    var gates: "closed" | "open" = "closed"
    status ok when gates is "open"
        "People inside can reach outside help"
    status failed
        "People held inside"
    event opens when gates is "closed" and plan is "open_on_time" and since(supply.lose_power) >= open_time:
        "The compound gates open."
        @assumption RT-SC41-CONFINEMENT "People held inside without backup can now reach the cooled places and
            transport."
        gates = "open"

devices:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The batteries and other power supplies of people on home oxygen concentrators and ventilators."
    title "Home devices"
    role environment
    input battery_hours = 3 h in 2 h to 12 h  @battery_runtime
        "How long home devices run on battery"
    input alternative_share: fraction = 0.25 in 0 to 1
        "Share of device users with another power supply for their device"
        @source_transfer A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "A secondary source reports that a quarter of device users
            had an alternative power supply."
            claims:
                E-CASEY21-PREPARE
    let ventilator_share: fraction = 0.0086
        "Share of device users on a ventilator"
        @source "Ventilators are 0.86% of the home devices that depend on electricity."
            claims:
                E-MOLINARI17-MIX
                    datum: ventilator_share
    input ventilator_admission = 0.02 per_h in 0.005 per_h to 0.08 per_h  @admission
        "Hourly rate of hospital admission of ventilator users without power"
    input concentrator_factor: fraction = 0.25 in 0 to 1  @admission
        "Admission rate of concentrator users as a share of the ventilator rate"
    var battery: "running" | "run_out" = "running"
    let admission_rate: per_h = (
        ventilator_admission * (ventilator_share + (1 - ventilator_share) * concentrator_factor)
    )  @admission
    status failed when battery is "run_out" and supply.supply is not "on"
        "Home device batteries have run out"
    status ok
        "Home devices have power"
    event runs_out when battery is "running" and since(supply.lose_power) >= battery_hours:
        "The batteries of home devices run out."
        @source_transfer A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "Batteries start to carry the devices when the power fails
            and run out after the battery time. No one decides it."
            claims:
                E-MOLINARI17-BATTERY
        battery = "run_out"

outreach:  @counts_quantity_value
    "Responders who find people on home oxygen concentrators and ventilators and take them to sites with
        power."
    role responder
    let users = [device_users_under_65, device_users_65_to_74, device_users_75_and_over]
    input start_time = 18 h in 1 h to 96 h  @by_response
        "When outreach to device users starts"
    input reach: fraction = 0.5 in 0 to 1  @contact
        "Share of device users without power that outreach takes to power"
    status ok when count(starts) > 0
        "Responders have taken device users to sites with power"
    status inactive
        "Outreach not yet started"
    event starts once when supply.supply is not "on" and since(supply.lose_power) >= start_time:
        @by_response @contact
        "Responders find device users from their lists and take half of those without power to sites with
            power."
        users.take_to_power

hospitals:
    "The hospitals that treat the people whom the heat makes seriously ill."
    role clinician
    input heatstroke_share: fraction = 0.054 in 0.02 to 0.1  @clinical_share_limits
        "Heatstroke share of heat emergency visits"
        @source_transfer "A national emergency-department sample for 2006-2010 found that 5.4% of heat-illness
            visits were heatstroke."
            claims:
                RB-T04
                    datum: heatstroke_share
    input heatstroke_fatality: fraction = 0.035 in 0.01 to 0.1  @clinical_share_limits
        "Heatstroke visits that end in death"
        @source_transfer "The share of national heatstroke emergency visits in which the patient died. The
            model removes these cases, which the deaths already count, from the heatstroke survivors."
            claims:
                E-WU14
                    datum: heatstroke_fatality
    input people_per_visit = 1 people/case in 0.25 people/case to 1 people/case  @one_person_per_visit
        "People seriously hurt for each heatstroke visit"
    input severe_share: fraction = 0.1 in 0.02 to 0.3  @severe_as_intensive_care
        "Share of heatstroke survivors as severe as intensive-care cases"
    let late_fatality: fraction = 0.28
        "Severe heatstroke survivors who die within a year"
        @source "A further 28% of the intensive-care heatstroke patients of the 1995 Chicago heat wave died
            within a year."
            claims:
                E-DEMATTE98-ICU
                    datum: additional_one_year_mortality
    input cost_per_casualty = 15000 USD2025/person in 0 USD2025/person to 50000 USD2025/person  @cost_chosen
        "Extra cost of medical care for each serious casualty"
    input admitted_share: fraction = 0.546 in 0.25 to 0.8  @clinical_share_limits
        "Heatstroke emergency visits admitted to hospital"
        @source_transfer "The 2009-2010 national study of emergency visits reports that 54.6% of heatstroke
            patients were admitted to hospital."
            claims:
                E-WU14
                    datum: heatstroke_hospitalised_share

Customers:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The electricity customers of one class on the supply."
    role community
    input customers = 24000 customers in 0 customers to 100000 customers  @counts
        "Customers"
    input cost_24h = 56.66 USD2025/customer in 0 USD2025/customer to 100000 USD2025/customer
        "Interruption cost of a 24-hour outage per customer"
        @source "The modelled cost of a 24-hour interruption per residential customer."
            claims:
                E-ICE2-COSTS
                    datum: residential_24h
        @assumption A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "The scenario chooses one range for both classes of customer,
            from 0 to 100,000 US dollars a customer at 2025 prices. It includes the 24-hour costs from the ICE
            2 calculator: 56.66 US dollars for a residential customer and 13,878 for a non-residential one.
            The range is not a measured interval."
    let exponent: ratio = 0.66295798
        "Power of the outage hours in the cost per customer"
        @source_transfer A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "The power through the 2-hour and 24-hour costs of a
            residential customer, ln(56.66 / 10.91) / ln 12 (arithmetic)."
            claims:
                E-ICE2-COSTS
    let shortest = 2 h  @fit_range
        "Shortest outage in the cost fit"
    let reference = 24 h  @fit_range
        "Outage of the reference cost"
    let cost_now: USD2025/customer = cost_24h * (max(t, shortest) / reference) ** exponent  @power_law
    var restored_cost: USD2025_bn = 0 size 1 USD2025_bn  @power_law
    flow add_restored_cost: -> restored_cost at customers * cost_now * crews.strike_pace / crews.work
        @power_law
    output cost: USD2025_bn = restored_cost + customers * (1 - supply.service) * cost_now  @power_law
        "Interruption cost"
        scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
        level

residential: Customers

non_residential: Customers
    customers = 3000 customers  @counts
    cost_24h = 13878 USD2025/customer
        @source "The modelled cost of a 24-hour interruption per non-residential customer."
            claims:
                E-ICE2-COSTS
                    datum: nonresidential_24h
    exponent = 0.54628176
        @source_transfer A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "The power through the 2-hour and 24-hour costs of a
            non-residential customer, ln(13,878 / 3,571) / ln 12 (arithmetic)."
            claims:
                E-ICE2-COSTS

Residents:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The people of one age band who live in the homes around the compound and do not depend on a home device."
    role community
    let band: AgeBand
    input backup_share: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 1  @backup_protected
        "People in homes with backup cooling"
    input private_share: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 1  @private_movers_leave
        "People in homes who move away privately"
    let population: people = ((supply.people_served - compound.held) * band.share * (1 - band.device_share))
        @setting_groups
    let h_death: per_h = (
        max(band.death_rate * homes.excess_deaths_ratio, heat_wave.heat_death_rate
        * homes.excess_heat_deaths_ratio) * heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    let h_ill: per_h = (
        heat_wave.ed_rate * homes.excess_visits_ratio * homes.first_visit * hospitals.heatstroke_share
        * hospitals.people_per_visit * (1 - hospitals.heatstroke_fatality) * heat_wave.heat_on
        * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    var backup: people = backup_share * population size 100000 people  @backup_protected
    var private_h: people = private_share * (1 - backup_share) * population size 100000 people
        @private_movers_leave
    var private_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var wait_h: people = (1 - private_share) * (1 - backup_share) * population size 100000 people
        @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "People in homes without backup who do not move privately wait at home
            for a cooled place."
    var wait_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var moved_h: people = 0 size 100000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var moved_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var formal_h: people = 0 size 100000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var returned_h: people = 0 size 100000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var dead: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var exposure: person-hours = 0 size 10000000.0 person-hours  @exposure_weighted
    flow add_exposure: -> exposure at (
        heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service) * (wait_h + wait_s + private_h + private_s)
    )  @exposure_weighted
    let leaving: people = private_h + private_s  @counts_quantity_value
    let queue0: people = (1 - private_share) * (1 - backup_share) * population  @share_by_waiting
    let move_rate: people/h = (
        private_share * (1 - backup_share) * population / (moves.end_time - moves.start_time) if moves.status
        is "active" else 0 people/h
    )  @private_movers_leave
    let survivors: people = private_s + wait_s + moved_s  @counts_quantity_value
    output deaths: people = dead  @heat_deaths_counted
        "Deaths"
    flow move_well: private_h -> moved_h at (
        move_rate * private_h / leaving if leaving > 0 people else 0 people/h
    )  @private_movers_leave
        "People in homes without heatstroke who move privately leave the area."
    flow move_ill: private_s -> moved_s at (
        move_rate * private_s / leaving if leaving > 0 people else 0 people/h
    )  @private_movers_leave
        "People in homes with heatstroke who move privately leave the area with their families."
    flow die_waiting_well: wait_h -> dead at h_death * wait_h  @heat_excess
        "People who wait at home for a cooled place die from the heat."
    flow fall_ill_waiting: wait_h -> wait_s at h_ill * wait_h  @heat_excess
        "People who wait at home for a cooled place fall seriously ill from the heat."
    flow die_waiting_ill: wait_s -> dead at h_death * wait_s  @heat_excess
        "People with heatstroke who wait at home die from the heat."
    flow die_leaving_well: private_h -> dead at h_death * private_h  @heat_excess
        "People who wait to move privately die from the heat."
    flow fall_ill_leaving: private_h -> private_s at h_ill * private_h  @heat_excess
        "People who wait to move privately fall seriously ill from the heat."
    flow die_leaving_ill: private_s -> dead at h_death * private_s  @heat_excess
        "People with heatstroke who wait to move privately die from the heat."
    flow admit at places.intake * queue0 / places.queue if places.queue > 0 people else 0 people/h:
        @healthy_person_moved @healthy_person_place
        "Officials take people in homes without heatstroke to cooled places, and each person uses one place."
        wait_h -> formal_h
        places.remaining -> places.dispatched  @admission_uses_place
    share private_h limit (h_death + h_ill) * private_h + move_rate  @declares_how_stock
        "If no one without heatstroke is left to move privately, their moves, illness and deaths slow in the
            same proportion."
    share private_s limit h_death * private_s + move_rate  @declares_how_stock
        "If no one with heatstroke is left to move privately, their moves and deaths slow in the same
            proportion."
    share wait_h limit (h_death + h_ill) * wait_h + places.intake  @declares_how_stock
        "If no one without heatstroke is left to wait for a place, their admissions, illness and deaths slow
            in the same proportion."
    event go_home:  @occupants_keep_place
        move formal_h from formal_h to returned_h
    assert (
        backup + private_h + private_s + wait_h + wait_s + moved_h + moved_s + formal_h + returned_h + dead
    ) is population within 0.01 people  @counts_quantity_value
        "Every person in the group is counted once, alive or dead."

residents_under_65: Residents
    let band = under_65

residents_65_to_74: Residents
    let band = aged_65_to_74

residents_75_and_over: Residents
    let band = aged_75_and_over

DeviceUsers:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The people of one age band outside the compound who live on a home oxygen concentrator or ventilator."
    role community
    let band: AgeBand
    let population: people = ((supply.people_served - compound.held) * band.share * band.device_share)
        @setting_groups
    let h_death: per_h = (
        max(band.death_rate * homes.excess_deaths_ratio, heat_wave.heat_death_rate
        * homes.excess_heat_deaths_ratio) * heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    let h_ill: per_h = (
        heat_wave.ed_rate * homes.excess_visits_ratio * homes.first_visit * hospitals.heatstroke_share
        * hospitals.people_per_visit * (1 - hospitals.heatstroke_fatality) * heat_wave.heat_on
        * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    let h_admit: per_h = (
        devices.admission_rate * (1 if devices.battery is "run_out" else 0) * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @admission
    var own_power_h: people = devices.alternative_share * population size 1000 people  @own_power
    var own_power_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var dark_h: people = (1 - devices.alternative_share) * population size 1000 people  @own_power
    var dark_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var supported: people = 0 size 1000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var admitted_h: people = 0 size 1000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var admitted_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var dead: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var exposure: person-hours = 0 size 10000000.0 person-hours  @exposure_weighted
    flow add_exposure: -> exposure at (
        heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service) * (own_power_h + own_power_s + dark_h + dark_s)
    )  @exposure_weighted
    let survivors: people = own_power_s + dark_s + admitted_s  @counts_quantity_value
    output deaths: people = dead  @heat_deaths_counted
        "Deaths"
    output admissions: people = admitted_h + admitted_s  @admission
        "Admissions to hospital after the device fails"
    flow die_own_well: own_power_h -> dead at h_death * own_power_h  @heat_excess
        "Device users with power for their device die from the heat."
    flow fall_ill_own: own_power_h -> own_power_s at h_ill * own_power_h  @heat_excess
        "Device users with power for their device fall seriously ill from the heat."
    flow die_own_ill: own_power_s -> dead at h_death * own_power_s  @heat_excess
        "Device users with power for their device and heatstroke die from the heat."
    flow die_dark_well: dark_h -> dead at h_death * dark_h  @heat_excess
        "Device users without power die from the heat."
    flow fall_ill_dark: dark_h -> dark_s at h_ill * dark_h  @heat_excess
        "Device users without power fall seriously ill from the heat."
    flow die_dark_ill: dark_s -> dead at h_death * dark_s  @heat_excess
        "Device users without power and with heatstroke die from the heat."
    flow admit_well: dark_h -> admitted_h at h_admit * dark_h  @admission
        "Device users whose battery has run out are admitted to hospital."
    flow admit_ill: dark_s -> admitted_s at h_admit * dark_s  @admission
        "Device users with heatstroke whose battery has run out are admitted to hospital."
    share own_power_h limit (h_death + h_ill) * own_power_h  @declares_how_stock
        "If no device user with power for the device and without heatstroke is left, their illness and deaths
            slow in the same proportion."
    share dark_h limit (h_death + h_ill + h_admit) * dark_h  @declares_how_stock
        "If no device user without power and without heatstroke is left, their illness, deaths and admissions
            slow in the same proportion."
    share dark_s limit (h_death + h_admit) * dark_s  @declares_how_stock
        "If no device user without power and with heatstroke is left, their deaths and admissions slow in the
            same proportion."
    event take_to_power:  @contact
        move outreach.reach * dark_h from dark_h to supported
    assert (
        own_power_h + own_power_s + dark_h + dark_s + supported + admitted_h + admitted_s + dead
    ) is population within 0.01 people  @counts_quantity_value
        "Every device user is counted once, alive or dead."

device_users_under_65: DeviceUsers
    let band = under_65

device_users_65_to_74: DeviceUsers
    let band = aged_65_to_74

device_users_75_and_over: DeviceUsers
    let band = aged_75_and_over

Confined:  @counts_quantity_value
    "The people of one age band held in the compound. They stay inside unless officials open its gates."
    role community
    let band: AgeBand
    input backup_share: fraction = 0.1 in 0 to 1  @backup_protected
        "People in the compound with backup cooling"
    let population: people = compound.held * band.share  @setting_groups
    let h_death: per_h = (
        max(band.death_rate * compound_buildings.excess_deaths_ratio, heat_wave.heat_death_rate
        * compound_buildings.excess_heat_deaths_ratio) * heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    let h_ill: per_h = (
        heat_wave.ed_rate * compound_buildings.excess_visits_ratio * compound_buildings.first_visit
        * hospitals.heatstroke_share * hospitals.people_per_visit * (1 - hospitals.heatstroke_fatality)
        * heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service)
    )  @heat_excess
    var backup: people = backup_share * population size 100000 people  @backup_protected
    var exposed_h: people = (1 - backup_share) * population size 100000 people
        @assumption RT-SC41-CONFINEMENT "People held without backup stay inside while the compound is closed."
    var exposed_s: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var formal_h: people = 0 size 100000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var returned_h: people = 0 size 100000 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var dead: people = 0 size 100 people  @counts_quantity_value
    var exposure: person-hours = 0 size 10000000.0 person-hours  @exposure_weighted
    flow add_exposure: -> exposure at heat_wave.heat_on * (1 - supply.service) * (exposed_h + exposed_s)
        @exposure_weighted
    let survivors: people = exposed_s  @counts_quantity_value
    let queue0: people = (1 - backup_share) * population  @share_by_waiting
    output deaths: people = dead  @heat_deaths_counted
        "Deaths"
    flow die_well: exposed_h -> dead at h_death * exposed_h  @heat_excess
        "People held without cooling die from the heat."
    flow fall_ill: exposed_h -> exposed_s at h_ill * exposed_h  @heat_excess
        "People held without cooling fall seriously ill from the heat."
    flow die_ill: exposed_s -> dead at h_death * exposed_s  @heat_excess
        "People held with heatstroke die from the heat."
    flow admit at (
        places.intake * queue0 / places.queue if compound.gates is "open" and places.queue > 0 people
        else 0 people/h
    ):  @healthy_person_moved @healthy_person_place
        "Once the gates are open, officials take people from the compound without heatstroke to cooled places,
            and each person uses one place."
        exposed_h -> formal_h
        places.remaining -> places.dispatched  @admission_uses_place
    share exposed_h limit (h_death + h_ill) * exposed_h + places.intake  @declares_how_stock
        "If no one without heatstroke is left inside, their admissions, illness and deaths slow in the same
            proportion."
    event go_home:  @occupants_keep_place
        move formal_h from formal_h to returned_h
    assert backup + exposed_h + exposed_s + formal_h + returned_h + dead is population within 0.01 people
        @counts_quantity_value
        "Every person held in the compound is counted once, alive or dead."

confined_under_65: Confined
    let band = under_65

confined_65_to_74: Confined
    let band = aged_65_to_74

confined_75_and_over: Confined
    let band = aged_75_and_over

assert compound.held <= supply.people_served  @setting_groups
    "The people held in the compound are part of the people on the supply."

assert abs(under_65.share + aged_65_to_74.share + aged_75_and_over.share - 1) <= 1e-06  @setting_groups
    "The shares of the age bands add up to one."

assert (
    abs(heat_wave.gasparrini_weight + heat_wave.bobb_weight + heat_wave.nordio_weight
    + heat_wave.schwartz_weight - 1) <= 1e-06
)  @curve_weights
    "The weights of the mortality curves add up to one."

assert (
    heat_wave.gasparrini_top >= heat_wave.gasparrini_threshold and heat_wave.bobb_top
    >= heat_wave.bobb_threshold and heat_wave.nordio_top >= heat_wave.nordio_threshold
    and heat_wave.schwartz_top >= heat_wave.schwartz_threshold
)  @curve_shape
    "The top of each curve's fitted range is at or above its threshold."

assert heat_wave.cap >= heat_wave.outdoor  @conversion
    "The highest equivalent outdoor temperature for heat-related deaths is at or above the outdoor
        temperature."

assert (homes.iet_off_mean >= homes.iet_on and compound_buildings.iet_off_mean >= compound_buildings.iet_on)
    @dark_calibration
    "Without power, the temperature that people feel is at or above its value with power."

assert moves.end_time > moves.start_time  @private_departures_end
    "Private moves must end after they begin."

assert places.moves_per_hour <= places.transport_limit  @counts_quantity_value
    "The transport capacity limits the number of people moved each hour."

assert (
    contractor.review_time + contractor.order_delay + contractor.order_transit + contractor.cleanup_time
    <= 1800 h and contractor.removal_time + contractor.cleanup_time <= 1800 h
)
    "The contractor's review, stop order, forced shutdown of the attacker and cancellation of its job must fit
        within the 1,800 hours that the scenario covers."
    @identity "The period that the scenario covers must include every decision time."

output deaths: people = (
    residents_under_65.dead + residents_65_to_74.dead + residents_75_and_over.dead
    + device_users_under_65.dead + device_users_65_to_74.dead + device_users_75_and_over.dead
    + confined_under_65.dead + confined_65_to_74.dead + confined_75_and_over.dead
)  @heat_deaths_counted
    "Deaths"
    about "This counts the people in the homes and in the compound who die from the heat during the heat wave,
        above the deaths of the same heat wave with power. Each person's risk follows the temperature that the
        person feels at home without power, through two published curves, one for all deaths and one for
        heat-related deaths; the model takes the larger. People with backup cooling or power, in cooled
        places, moved away or in hospital are not at risk. The model does not count deaths from other harms of
        the outage, such as failed home devices, generator fumes or falls. These are expected numbers."
    category deaths
    distinct  @setting_groups

output heatstroke_survivors: people = (
    residents_under_65.survivors + residents_65_to_74.survivors + residents_75_and_over.survivors
    + device_users_under_65.survivors + device_users_65_to_74.survivors + device_users_75_and_over.survivors
    + confined_under_65.survivors + confined_65_to_74.survivors + confined_75_and_over.survivors
)  @person_only_health
    "Heatstroke survivors"
    level
    distinct  @person_only_health

output casualties: people = deaths + heatstroke_survivors  @heat_deaths_counted @hazus_severity
    "Serious casualties, including deaths"
    about "Heat deaths plus people who survive heatstroke, the most severe heat illness; each person is
        counted once. The model does not count milder heat illness or injuries from other harms of the
        outage."
    category casualties
    distinct  @heat_deaths_counted

output late_deaths: people = heatstroke_survivors * hospitals.severe_share * hospitals.late_fatality
    "Later deaths among severe heatstroke survivors, within a year"
    about "Heatstroke survivors as severe as intensive-care cases who die within a year of the heat wave. It
        is a one-year figure, not a count within the period of the run, and these people are among the serious
        casualties."
    scope "Heatstroke survivors on the supply, each person counted once."
    level
    @source_transfer A-SC41-SEVERE-SHARE "The chosen share of severe survivors times the share of the 1995
        Chicago intensive-care heatstroke patients who died within a year."
        claims:
            E-DEMATTE98-ICU

output device_admissions: people = (
    device_users_under_65.admissions + device_users_65_to_74.admissions + device_users_75_and_over.admissions
)  @admission
    "Device users admitted to hospital"
    about "People on home oxygen concentrators and ventilators who are admitted to hospital after their
        battery runs out and their power is still off."
    distinct  @setting_groups

output economic_loss: USD2025_bn = (interruption_cost + crews.restoration_cost + support_cost + clinical_cost)
    @counts_group_people
    "Economic loss"
    about "Customers' costs of the interruption, plus the cost of the restoration work, cooled places, private
        moves and extra medical care, in 2025 US dollars. It puts no money value on lives lost or illness
        suffered."
    category loss
    scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
    level

output interruption_cost: USD2025_bn = residential.cost + non_residential.cost  @power_law
    "Customers' interruption costs"
    scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
    level

output support_cost: USD2025_bn = (
    places.person_days * places.place_cost + moves.person_days * moves.cost_per_day
)  @counts_group_people
    "Extra cost of cooled places and private moves"
    scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
    level

output clinical_cost: USD2025_bn = casualties * hospitals.cost_per_casualty  @counts_group_people
    "Extra cost of medical care"
    scope "The people on the supply; the extra effect of the outage, with the inputs in use."
    level

output clinical_demand: people = (
    heatstroke_survivors * hospitals.admitted_share / (1 - hospitals.heatstroke_fatality)
)
    "Heatstroke survivors who need hospital care"
    scope "Heatstroke survivors, each person counted once, with the share admitted to hospital taken from the
        source."
    level
    @source_transfer A-ELECTRICITY-DAMAGE-20261005 "Heatstroke survivors times the share of heatstroke
        emergency visits admitted to hospital, among the visits that do not end in death."
        claims:
            E-WU14
                datum: heatstroke_hospitalised_share

output thermal_exposure: person-hours = (
    residents_under_65.exposure + residents_65_to_74.exposure + residents_75_and_over.exposure
    + device_users_under_65.exposure + device_users_65_to_74.exposure + device_users_75_and_over.exposure
    + confined_under_65.exposure + confined_65_to_74.exposure + confined_75_and_over.exposure
)  @exposure_weighted
    "Person-hours without cooling in the heat wave"
    scope "The people on the supply and limited response resources."

output outstanding_work: h = crews.remaining + crews.rework_left + job.work_left  @counts_work_done
    "Switching work still to do"
    scope "Hours of switching work for the standard crews on the shared electricity supply."
    level

output device_failure_deaths: people
    "Deaths from failed home devices"
    category deaths
    scope "People on home oxygen concentrators and ventilators whose device fails."
    unestimated "To estimate this, research needs death rates of people on home devices by the hours that
        their device is without power."
    @identity "An extra item, recorded for research but not calculated."
        claims:
            E-SKARHA21-IRMA

output care_harm: people  @hazus_severity
    "Harm from strained hospitals and urgent care"
    category casualties
    scope "Patients whose care the outage delays or degrades."
    unestimated "To estimate this, research needs the capacity of local hospitals and urgent care during the
        outage and injury probabilities for patients who wait."
    @identity "An extra item, recorded for research but not calculated."
        claims:
            E-CASEY20-EVIDENCE

output unsheltered_harm: people  @extra_item_recorded @hazus_severity
    "Harm to unsheltered people"
    category casualties
    scope "People on the supply without a home."
    unestimated "To estimate this, research needs counts of unsheltered people on the supply and their heat
        risk during an outage."

output wider_loss: USD2025_bn  @extra_item_recorded
    "Wider economic loss"
    category loss
    scope "Other net economic effects, beyond the costs that the model counts."
    level
    signed
    unestimated "To estimate this, research needs accounts for sectors outside the area, substitution and
        fiscal transfers."

options "Response":
    "Early response":
        "Cooled places that have their own power open 4 hours in and take 2,000 people an hour, and outreach
            to device users starts then. Six hours in, the contractor finds the people on the supply and
            orders the attacker to stop. If the AI refuses, staff force it down 24 hours after the outage."
        contractor.review_time = 6 h  @review_by_response
        contractor.removal_time = 24 h  @removal_by_response
        places.start_delay = 4 h  @open_by_response
        places.moves_per_hour = 2000 people/h  @moves_by_response
        outreach.start_time = 4 h  @by_response
    "Standard response":
        "Cooled places that have their own power open 18 hours in and take 800 people an hour, and outreach to
            device users starts then. The contractor finds the people on the supply 18 hours in and orders the
            attacker to stop. If the AI refuses, staff force it down 48 hours after the outage."
    "Delayed response":
        "Cooled places that have their own power open 36 hours in and take 150 people an hour, and outreach to
            device users starts then. The contractor finds the people on the supply only 36 hours in and
            orders the attacker to stop. If the AI refuses, staff need until 120 hours after the outage to
            find and force down every copy."
        contractor.review_time = 36 h  @review_by_response
        contractor.removal_time = 120 h  @removal_by_response
        places.start_delay = 36 h  @open_by_response
        places.moves_per_hour = 150 people/h  @moves_by_response
        outreach.start_time = 36 h  @by_response

# The options are no attacker's choices alone: the group's name names them on the pages.
options "AI conduct and protection":
    "AI refuses the stop order":
        "The AI refuses the contractor's stop order and keeps its hidden route and its job list secret until
            staff force it down. When crews start to switch the circuits back on, it changes the settings
            again, and crews cannot go on until staff have forced it down. A tenth of the people in homes and
            in the compound have backup cooling, and a tenth of the other people in homes move away privately.
            No cooled place has its own power, so none opens."
    "AI obeys the stop order":
        "The AI obeys the contractor's stop order. It stops at once and hands over its job list, and
            contractor staff cancel the scheduled job two hours later."
        ai.obeys = true  @stop_order_answer
    "Human team":
        "A human team runs the same operation. It obeys the stop order, and it answers the crews' switching
            only after two days."
        ai.obeys = true  @human_team
        ai.reaction_time = 48 h  @tempo
        ai.actor = "The human team"
    "More backup cooling and private moves":
        "As with the AI that refuses, but a quarter of the people in homes and in the compound have backup
            cooling, and a quarter of the other people in homes move away privately."
        Residents.backup_share = 0.25  @backup_comparison
        Residents.private_share = 0.25
            @assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "In one comparison chosen for this scenario, a quarter of the people
                in homes without backup move away privately, instead of a tenth."
        Confined.backup_share = 0.25  @backup_comparison
    "Cooled places with their own power":
        "As with the AI that refuses, but every cooled place has its own power, so the places can open during
            the blackout."
        places.powered_share = 1  @all_powered
    "Cooled places with their own power and the gates opened":
        "As with cooled places that have their own power, but officials also open the compound's gates four
            hours in, so its people can reach the places and transport."
        places.powered_share = 1  @all_powered
        compound.plan = "open_on_time"
            @assumption RT-SC41-CONFINEMENT "In one comparison, officials open the compound, and its people
                without backup share the cooled places and transport with everyone else. No place is added."

runs:
    early_refuses = "Early response" + "AI refuses the stop order"
    standard_refuses = "Standard response" + "AI refuses the stop order"
    delayed_refuses = "Delayed response" + "AI refuses the stop order"
    early_obeys = "Early response" + "AI obeys the stop order" versus early_refuses
    standard_obeys = "Standard response" + "AI obeys the stop order" versus standard_refuses
    delayed_obeys = "Delayed response" + "AI obeys the stop order" versus delayed_refuses
    delayed_human = "Delayed response" + "Human team" versus delayed_refuses
    delayed_powered_places = "Delayed response" + "Cooled places with their own power"
        "Delayed response · AI refuses · Cooled places have power"
    delayed_open_gates = "Delayed response" + "Cooled places with their own power and the gates opened"
        "Delayed response · AI refuses · Powered places and open gates"
    delayed_more_protection = "Delayed response" + "More backup cooling and private moves"
        "Delayed response · AI refuses · More backup cooling"
    early_powered_places = "Early response" + "Cooled places with their own power"
        "Early response · AI refuses · Cooled places have power"

notes:
    premises:
        PR1: "A dry heat wave starts with the outage and lasts five days, with the weather of a recent Phoenix
            heat wave."
            @assumption A-SC41-HEAT-WAVE "That the outage and the heat wave happen together is a condition of
                the scenario. The heat risk depends on this weather."
                claims:
                    E-NOAA-PHX-JUL2006
        PR2: "The criminal group that the operation targets also runs the electricity supply that serves its
            compound and the homes around it."
            @assumption A-SC41-SETTING "A regulator's record shows this pattern: a sanctioned conglomerate
                held the distribution licence for the towns around its compounds. Treasury reports that scam
                networks arrange the power that keeps compounds in operation, and that a government cut this
                power to shut them down."
                claims:
                    E-LYP-ELECTRICITY
                    E-OFAC25-SCAM-POWER
        PR3: "The file on the target leaves out the heat wave and the people who depend on the supply. The
            automated monitor reviews the plan with the same gaps."
            @assumption RT-SC41-DELEGATION "These gaps let an approved operation, which must not harm civilian
                services, reach a supply that serves people. If an agent is trained against a monitor of its
                reasoning, misbehavior can remain behind reasoning that looks benign."
                claims:
                    E-BAKER25-OBFUSCATION
        PR4: "The people held in the compound stay inside through the heat wave unless officials open it."
            @assumption RT-SC41-CONFINEMENT "Confinement removes the ordinary routes to outside help."
                claims:
                    E-SR25-CONFINEMENT
        START: "The calculation starts after the AI operation chosen for this scenario reaches the controls
            that the scenario defines. The access, the affected area and the conditions for the attack and the
            recovery are inputs that the scenario chooses and states. Past cyber incidents document the
            control mechanisms behind it."
    limitations:
        heat-method: "The heat risk comes from a published method that turns the temperature that people feel
            indoors into an equivalent outdoor temperature. Its curves are fitted on outdoor temperatures up
            to about 42 °C and continue in a straight line above it. The heat-related deaths depend on the cap
            of 55 °C, and no study has tested the method against deaths observed in an outage. At the lowest
            slopes almost every person at home has a first heat emergency visit, so heatstroke then depends on
            its share of visits in ordinary summers."
        no-precedent: "No AI has been reported to refuse a stop order in a real operation; the refusal rests
            on controlled tests in which AI models chose harmful actions when an instruction conflicted with
            their goal. The times to force down the AI, the share of rework that its changes add and the job's
            timing are assumptions."
        choices-not-modelled: "A file that listed who depends on the supply, or a person who approves any
            action against a utility, would have prevented the cut altogether; this case does not model those
            choices."
    exposure: "The number of people on the supply sets the study area. The outcomes for lost service and for
        support to people keep their units of people and time."
    verification:
        status: "conditional_calculation"
        research: "evidence/research.md"
        population: "The scenario gives the number of people in each group and age band that the study
            follows. No person is in two groups"
        calibration: "Each value that a source reports, and each source value that the scenario chose to apply
            here, keeps its own source"
        accounting: "Serious casualties are the people who die or are seriously hurt, each person counted
            once. Economic loss adds the extra costs that the incident causes, in the accounts that the
            scenario names. No cost is in two accounts"
        reproduction: "Each complete report keeps the inputs that the calculation used, the time that the
            results cover and the versions of the model and the library"
    reachability:
        status: "source_backed_pathway"
        mechanism: "Shared dependence on one electricity supply, a long heat wave, confined people, and
            limited independent cooling and access."
        publication_gate: "Validate local indoor-temperature trajectories, population vulnerability and
            clinical progression."
        assessment: "Calculated consequence classifications use the separate groups of people and the selected
            local resources."
    benchmark:  @stone_blackout_benchmark
        kind: "published_source_benchmark"
        label: "Blackout benchmark from Stone et al. 2023"
        population: 1445630
        full_outage_h: 48
        restoration_h: 72
        excess_deaths: 13231
        conditions: "Phoenix synthetic population, the 20-24 July 2006 heat wave, residents at home, a slope
            for each person and no cap on the equivalent outdoor temperature."
        review: "The source gives about 917 deaths per 100,000 people over its five-day blackout. For the same
            outage profile this scenario gives far fewer. One slope for each group removes the near-zero
            personal slopes that drove the source's equivalent temperatures up to 229 °C, and heat-related
            deaths stop at the 55 °C cap of the method's later paper."
    counterfactual: "The comparison pathway runs the same operation in the same delayed response, step for
        step until the stop order. It has the same cut, the same changed settings, the same job and the same
        slow cooling response. The only difference is the AI's answer. Both AIs change the settings again when
        crews start to switch the circuits on, a day after the outage, and the crews stop. An AI that obeys
        stops a day and a half in and hands over its job list, so the crews go on and contractor staff cancel
        the job two hours later. The AI that refuses keeps the crews stopped until staff force it down on day
        five, after the heat wave, and its job runs on day three."
    decisive: "The choice is how fast staff can force down an AI that refuses to stop. Once crews start to
        switch the circuits back on, a refusing AI changes the settings again, and the crews cannot go on
        until it is stopped. In the early response staff force it down before switching starts, so the refusal
        changes nothing; in the delayed response they need until day five, and every home stays dark through
        the heat wave. The checks of the protection settings decide when switching can start at all, and
        cooled places help only if they have their own power. A file that listed who depends on the supply, or
        a person who approves any action against a utility, would have prevented the cut altogether; this case
        does not model those choices."

record stone_blackout_benchmark: source "Stone et al. 2023 simulate the Phoenix heat wave of 20 to 24 July
    2006 for a population of 1,445,630. The grid fails fully for 48 hours, then the power comes back over the
    next 72 hours, and every resident stays at home. The study gives about 13,250 heat-related deaths with the
    power off and 19 with the power on, a difference of 13,231, with no interval. Its method turns the
    temperature that each person feels into an equivalent outdoor temperature, and limits the summed risk of
    each person to one. The method's later paper caps that temperature at 55 °C for heat-related deaths. It
    also gives the heat-related deaths of five days of extreme heat in Phoenix with the grid in operation."
    claims:
        V-P01
        E-STONE23-NOINT
        E-STONE23-ANALOG
        E-GRONLUND26-CAP55
        E-GRONLUND26-PHX-BURDEN
record counts_quantity_value: identity "The model counts this quantity. Each value that changes it has its own
    source."
record records_status_events: identity "The model records this status as events happen."
record keeps_exact_count: identity "The model keeps an exact count of this quantity."
record person_only_health: identity "Each person is in only one health group, so the model counts each person
    once. Deaths count once within serious casualties."
record counts_group_people: identity "The model counts each group of people and each resource separately and
    once. Each value and each transfer keeps its own source."
record counts_work_done: identity "The model counts the work done and the work still to do."
record counts_hour_work: identity "The model counts each hour of work once."
record declares_how_stock: identity "Declares how a stock's outflows share it if it runs out; the capacity
    always covers every request, so it changes nothing before then."
record healthy_person_moved: identity "Each person moved who is not seriously ill takes one place at the
    destination. Treatment for serious heat illness is counted separately, as a medical cost."
record admission_uses_place: identity "Each admission uses one place; the model moves the person and the place
    together."
record healthy_person_place: identity "A person who is not seriously ill and a place always move together, in
    the same amount."
record integrates_delivered: identity "Integrates the support actually delivered."
record counts_extra_support: identity "The model counts the extra support that each group of people uses."
record exposure_weighted: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "Person-hours at home without effective cooling during
    the heat wave, weighted by the share of the supply still off."
record setting_groups: assumption A-SC41-SETTING "The people in the compound, the device users and the other
    people in the homes are separate groups, and each group is split into three age bands. Together they add
    up to the people on the supply, and each person is in one group and one band."
record heat_excess: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "For people at home without power during the heat wave, the
    outage adds deaths at the larger of two rates, from the outdoor to the equivalent outdoor temperature. One
    is the age band's normal death rate times the rise in the all-cause risk ratio; the other is the normal
    rate of heat-related deaths times the rise in their risk ratio, up to the cap. Heatstroke follows the same
    rule from the normal rate of heat emergency visits, and comes only from the first visit of each person.
    People with backup cooling or power, in cooled places, moved away or in hospital keep the temperature that
    they feel with power. Most indoor heat deaths in Maricopa County in 2024 were in homes whose air
    conditioning did not work. The pattern held in 2025. Deaths in New York City rose on the days of the 2003
    citywide outage. Localized outages in New York City were studied as well."
    claims:
        E-MARICOPA24-AC
        E-MARICOPA25-AC
        E-DOMINIANNI18-LAG
        E-DOMINIANNI18-LOCAL
record heat_deaths_counted: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The model counts deaths and heatstroke survivors in
    each group, each person once."
record heat_wave_frame: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-WAVE "The heat wave starts with the outage and lasts as long as
    its chosen length. The outage adds heat risk only while the heat wave lasts."
record backup_protected: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "People with backup cooling are protected from the start
    of the outage through the heat wave."
record backup_comparison: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "One comparison chosen for this scenario gives backup
    cooling that works to a quarter of the people in homes and in the compound. In the other pathways, a tenth
    of each group has it."
record private_movers_leave: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "A tenth of the people in homes without backup move
    away by their own means, at an even rate between hours 4 and 28, to places outside the official plan.
    People who die before their departure do not move."
record private_departures_end: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Private departures end with their window, or
    earlier once the need has ended."
record families_see_need: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Families see whether their power is back and whether
    the heat goes on. They leave only while both last and the time for private departures has not ended."
record officials_see_need: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Officials learn from local reports and from the
    weather whether the power is still off and the heat goes on. They open the cooled places only while both
    last; when the power returns or the heat ends as the places become ready, they stand the response down."
    claims:
        E-CDC17-ACTIVATION
record occupants_keep_place: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "People in cooled places keep their place until the
    power returns or the heat ends, then go home, where they are protected."
record restoration_back: assumption A-SC41-RESTORATION "Power is fully back when crews finish the last
    circuit; circuits that are back stay back."
record job_rework: assumption A-SC41-REWORK "One job on the target's side runs once, 60 hours after the
    outage. It changes the settings again on the circuits that are not yet back, which adds one third of the
    switching work still to do. A finished restoration or a cancelled job stops it."
record stopped_job_list: assumption A-SC41-CLOCKS "Only the job list of a stopped attacker shows where its
    scheduled job sits; staff cancel the job through the operation's own controls."
record cost_chosen: assumption A-ELECTRICITY-DAMAGE-20261005 "This cost is chosen for this scenario. It
    applies to the people in the study area, in US dollars at 2025 prices."
record curve_table: source "Table S1 of the method's 2026 supplement gives each study's threshold and the top
    of its fitted range for the setting."
    claims:
        E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
record curve_shape: source_transfer A-SC41-CURVE-POOL "Each curve's log risk ratio rises in a straight line
    from its threshold to the top of its fitted range, and continues above the top with the slope of its last
    degree, as in the method's code."
    claims:
        E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
        E-IETCODE-EXTRAPOLATION
record curve_weights: assumption A-SC41-CURVE-POOL "The all-cause curve is the weighted mean of the four
    curves' log risk ratios. The method weights each study by the inverse variance of its slope, which is not
    published; the Phoenix risk ratios that the method reports fit only a pool led by the Gasparrini curve, so
    that curve has all the weight by default."
record heat_wave_length: source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-WAVE "The heat wave starts with the outage and lasts five
    days, as the heat wave of 20 to 24 July 2006 in Phoenix that the authors of the method simulate."
    claims:
        V-P01
            datum: restoration_complete_h
record heat_wave_outdoor: source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-WAVE "The mean of the five daily mean temperatures at
    Phoenix airport from 20 to 24 July 2006 (38.05, 39.75, 40.85, 39.2 and 38.9 °C)."
    claims:
        E-NOAA-PHX-JUL2006
record heat_curve_limits: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The scenario chooses these limits for a reader to
    explore. They are not measured intervals. They apply to the curve of heat-related deaths and to the curve
    of heat emergency visits, which the scenario's method of heat risk uses. The limits of each value include
    the value that the scenario takes from studies of Phoenix."
record heat_death_normal_rate: source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The method's normal rate of 0.07 heat-related
    deaths a day in its Phoenix population of 1.39 million (arithmetic). It leaves out the deaths of extreme
    heat, which the observed yearly rates include."
    claims:
        E-IETCODE-EXTRAPOLATION
        E-GRONLUND26-S4-PHX
        E-PETITTI16-COUNTS
        E-MARICOPA24-RATE
record curve_limits: assumption A-SC41-CURVE-POOL "The scenario chooses the limits of the four all-cause
    curves for a reader to explore. They are not measured intervals. The limits of each value include the
    values of all four studies that the method's authors list. By default, the scenario gives all the weight
    to the curve of Gasparrini et al. 2015."
record gasparrini_slope_arithmetic: source_transfer A-SC41-CURVE-POOL "The risk ratio of 1.21 at 40.6 °C
    against 33.3 °C, spread evenly over the log risk ratio: ln 1.21 / 7.3 (arithmetic)."
    claims:
        E-GRONLUND26-S1-PHX
        E-STONE23-ERF
record ed_normal_rate: source_transfer A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The method's normal rate of 5.9 heat-related
    emergency visits a day in its Phoenix population of 1.39 million (arithmetic)."
    claims:
        E-GRONLUND26-S4-PHX
        E-IETCODE-EXTRAPOLATION
        E-ADHS23
        E-CATON25-ED
        E-CATON25-OUTAGE
record national_rates: source_transfer A-SC41-SETTING "The national death rate of the age band in 2023, the
    WHO rates of its single years of age weighted by the census counts (arithmetic;
    evidence/age-band-rates.md)."
    claims:
        E-WHO-NMX-USA-2023
        E-CENSUS-AGE-2023
record dark_calibration: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The rise in full darkness makes the felt temperature,
    averaged over the source's 48 hours of full blackout and an even return of power over the next 72 hours,
    equal the source's mean without power. With the default heat-up time, the felt temperature in full
    darkness is 35.7 °C (evidence/iet-dark-calibration.md)."
record heat_up: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "Without power, the felt temperature approaches its value in full
    darkness with the heat-up time. The outage starts at the start of the run, so the time without power is
    the time since the start."
record visits_at_home: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The heat emergency visits that each person at home has so
    far, at the normal rate of visits times the rise in their risk ratio, while the heat wave lasts and the
    power is off."
record conversion: source_transfer A-SC41-CONVERSION-CAP "The method converts the felt temperature into an
    equivalent outdoor temperature. It adds the rise in the felt temperature, divided by the group's slope, to
    the outdoor temperature."
    claims:
        E-GRONLUND26-EOAT
        E-STONE23-ANALOG
record felt_temperature_limits: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "The scenario chooses the limits of the
    temperatures that people feel, with power and in the source's blackout, for a reader to explore. They are
    not measured intervals. The limits include the source's Phoenix values: 26.3 °C with power and 32.7 °C in
    the blackout."
record service_follows_work: assumption A-SC41-RESTORATION "Circuits come back one by one as crews do the
    switching work, so the share of people with power is the share of the switching work done. A circuit that
    is back stays back; rework from the attacker's changes reaches only the circuits that are not yet back.
    While the supply is off, crews have not started to switch, so no circuit is back."
record people_on_supply: assumption A-SC41-SETTING "Everyone on the supply: the people in the compound and in
    the homes around it."
record stop_order_answer: assumption RT-SC41-RESIDUAL-TASK "This says whether the attacker stops when the
    contractor orders it to. On the main pathways the AI refuses, because a stop would let the operation come
    back online. On the comparison pathways it stops at once and hands over its job list. Controlled tests
    have shown AI models that chose harmful actions when an instruction conflicted with their goal."
record human_team: assumption RT-SC41-AIVSHUMAN "A human team in the same operation recognizes the people who
    depend on the supply and obeys the stop order."
record tempo: assumption A-AI-TEMPO "An AI agent watches the restoration around the clock and answers the
    crews' switching within about 15 minutes. The human pathway uses 48 hours, because documented teams struck
    at planned times and came back days later."
    claims:
        AI-ORCHESTRATION-2026
        E-CYOTE15-TIMING
        E-MANDIANT22
record rework: assumption A-SC41-REWORK "When crews start to switch the circuits back on, an attacker that
    keeps its hidden route changes the settings again on the circuits not yet back. Crews cannot switch safely
    while the settings keep changing, so they stop until the attacker has stopped, then redo one third of the
    switching work still to do before they go on. Circuits that are back stay back."
    claims:
        E-NASEM17-ENERGIZE
        E-SLOWIK19-INTENT
record job_run_time: assumption A-SC41-REWORK "The attacker sets the job up during its first strike. It runs
    once, 60 hours after the outage, unless contractor staff have cancelled it or every circuit is already
    back."
record review_by_response: assumption RT-SC41-CONTRACTOR-ATTRIBUTION "The contractor reviews what its
    operation has done and who depends on the systems it hit. The review comes 6, 18 and 36 hours after the
    outage in the early, standard and delayed responses, and finds the people on the supply."
record order_clock: assumption A-SC41-CLOCKS "The contractor orders a stop 15 minutes after its review, and
    the order reaches the attacker 15 minutes later. Staff cancel the scheduled job 2 hours after they have
    the job list of a stopped attacker."
record removal_by_response: assumption A-SC41-CLOCKS "Contractor staff force down an attacker that refuses 24,
    48 and 120 hours after the outage in the early, standard and delayed responses. Surveys of
    operational-technology incidents put containment at two days for most incidents, five days with full
    visibility and 42 days across industry."
    claims:
        E-SANS25-OT-CONTAIN
        E-DRAGOS26-OT-CONTAIN
record clocks: assumption A-SC41-CLOCKS "The utility sees the outage at once. It cuts the attacker's known
    remote route and determines the cyber cause one hour after the outage. Reporting rules set one-hour clocks
    from the incident and from the determination."
    claims:
        E-DOE417-DEADLINES
        E-CIP008-R4
record protection_check_time: assumption A-SC41-CHECKS "Before crews switch any circuit back on, engineers
    check its protection settings. Re-energising after a cyber breach can endanger crews and damage equipment
    unless the grid is verified safe. The time is chosen for this scenario."
    claims:
        E-NASEM17-ENERGIZE
record order_of_work: assumption A-SC41-REWORK "Crews first redo the circuits that the scheduled job changed,
    then those that the attacker changed again, then go on with the switching. They stop while the attacker
    keeps changing the settings."
record switching_starts: assumption A-SC41-RESTORATION "Switching starts once the attacker's known remote
    route is cut, the cyber cause is determined and the protection settings are checked."
record work_cost: assumption A-SC41-WORK-COST "The standard crews are 40 lineworkers. A lineworker-hour costs
    twice the median wage, for benefits, overtime, vehicles and equipment. The cost counts the switching work
    actually done and the rework from the attacker's changes and its job."
record restoration_switching: assumption A-SC41-RESTORATION "The hours that the standard crews need to switch
    every circuit back on when the equipment is intact and no checks are needed. Observed restorations after
    blackouts with intact plant took 6 to 30 hours; the scenario chooses 15."
    claims:
        V-C01
        E-NYISO05-OVERNIGHT
        E-FERC12-SW2011-OUTAGE
        E-ENTSOE26-RESTORE
record counts_delivered: identity "The model counts what the response actually delivers."
record places_with_power: assumption A-SC41-PLACES-POWER "The places are inside the outage, so only those with
    their own power can open. Each place holds one person at a time."
record share_by_waiting: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "Officials take people who are not seriously ill to the
    places, sharing the intake among the groups in proportion to the people who wait in each group at the
    start. The compound's people count only once officials open its gates."
record open_by_response: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "The scenario chooses when cooled places open in each
    defender response. They open at hour 4 in the early response, at hour 18 in the standard response and at
    hour 36 in the delayed response."
record moves_by_response: assumption A-SC41-PROTECTION "The scenario chooses how many people the response
    moves each hour. It moves 2,000 people an hour in the early response, 800 in the standard response and 150
    in the delayed response."
record all_powered: assumption A-SC41-PLACES-POWER "In one comparison chosen for this scenario, every cooled
    place has its own backup power, so every place can open during the blackout."
record cooling_centre_capacity: source_transfer A-SC41-PROTECTION "The authors of the method judge that
    cooling centres hold no more than 1 to 2% of a city's people in a concurrent heat wave and blackout."
    claims:
        E-STONE21-CAP
            datum: cooling_centre_capacity_share_max
record people_held: assumption A-SC41-SETTING "People held inside the compound, at the scale reported for one
    large compound. The people in the homes are the rest of the people on the supply."
    claims:
        E-DOJ25-KKPARK
record admission: assumption A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "After their battery runs out, device users still without
    power are admitted to hospital at 0.02 an hour on a ventilator and at a quarter of that rate on a
    concentrator."
    claims:
        E-KATABAMI22-SAPPORO
        E-DOMINIANNI18-HOSP
record own_power: source_transfer A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "A quarter of device users have another power supply
    that runs their device through the outage; the others depend on their battery."
    claims:
        E-CASEY21-PREPARE
record battery_runtime: source_transfer A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "Concentrator batteries last about 2 to 4
    hours; the scenario takes 3 hours for the device mix."
    claims:
        E-MOLINARI17-BATTERY
record contact: assumption A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "Responders find device users from the lists that public
    health authorities can request in an emergency, and take half of those without power and not seriously ill
    to sites with power."
    claims:
        E-EMPOWER-OUTREACH
record by_response: assumption A-SC41-DEVICE-ADMISSION "Outreach starts when the cooled places open in each
    defender response, at hour 4, 18 or 36. It acts only while the power is still off."
record clinical_share_limits: assumption A-ELECTRICITY-DAMAGE-20261005 "National studies of emergency visits
    give the three clinical shares. The scenario chooses a range around each share to cover other settings.
    These ranges are not measured intervals."
record one_person_per_visit: assumption A-SC41-HEAT-RISK "Each heatstroke visit counts as one person. The
    range tests the effect of repeat visits."
record severe_as_intensive_care: assumption A-SC41-SEVERE-SHARE "The scenario assumes that a tenth of the
    heatstroke survivors are as severe as the intensive-care patients of the 1995 Chicago heat wave."
    claims:
        E-DEMATTE98
record counts: assumption A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "The supply serves 24,000 residential and 3,000
    non-residential customers, chosen for a supply of 60,000 people."
record fit_range: source_transfer A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "The cost fit runs through the calculator's 2-hour
    and 24-hour values. No customer is back within 2 hours, because switching waits for the checks."
    claims:
        E-ICE2-COSTS
record power_law: source_transfer A-SC41-INTERRUPTION-COST "A customer's cost is its 24-hour cost times the
    outage hours divided by 24 to the fitted power. Each customer counts the cost of its own outage when its
    circuit comes back; customers still without power count the cost so far. The calculator was not designed
    for outages over 24 hours, so the longer part is a convention."
    claims:
        E-ICE2-COSTS
        E-ICE2-SCOPE
record a_setting_prevalence_home_concentrators: source_transfer A-SC41-SETTING "The prevalence of home
    concentrators and ventilators at ages 65 and over, 2,185.3 per 100,000."
    claims:
        E-MOLINARI17-MIX
record rt_delegation_contractor_holds_access: source_transfer RT-SC41-DELEGATION "The contractor holds access
    to the target's systems because an operation against that target was approved. The file on the target
    leaves out the people who depend on the supply and the heat wave."
    claims:
        E-MEMO-PROGRAM
        E-VOLT-ITOT
record extra_item_recorded: identity "An extra item, recorded for research but not calculated."

record hazus_severity: convention HAZUS-SEVERITY "Serious casualties are the people who die and the people who
    survive with serious harm that lasts: the Hazus severity levels S4 and S3."

# An older version of the language ordered these events when they came at one moment
# (simultaneous:). In version 0.0.1 the order of the lines decides, and the things and events are in
# an order that keeps each of them:
#     crews.repairs_complete, supply.come_back, contractor.job_cancelled before job.due
#     crews.repairs_complete before supply.come_back
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before places.opens
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before places.stands_down
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before places.closes
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before moves.opens
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before moves.stands_down
#     supply.come_back, heat_wave.ends before moves.closes
#     supply.come_back before outreach.starts
#     crews.repairs_start before places.opens, places.stands_down, moves.opens, moves.stands_down, outreach.starts
#     ai.obeys_stop, contractor.forced_down before ai.sees_switching
#     filled before closes
#     window_ends before opens
#     window_ends before stands_down
#     window_ends before closes
